WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.428
Euclid's Elements, Book One, Proposition Forty.

2
00:00:03.428 --> 00:00:08.419
Equal triangles on equal bases and on the same side lie in the same parallels.

3
00:00:08.419 --> 00:00:17.442
Let triangles A B C and C D E be equal in area, standing on equal bases B C and C E on the same straight line B E and on the same side of it.

4
00:00:17.442 --> 00:00:22.370
We prove that the segment A D joining their vertices is parallel to base B E.

5
00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.523
Join point A to point D with segment A D.

6
00:00:25.523 --> 00:00:28.922
Suppose segment A D is not parallel to segment B E.

7
00:00:28.922 --> 00:00:38.920
Then by Proposition Thirty-One, through point A draw segment A F parallel to segment B E, meeting segment C D at point F, and join point F to point E.

8
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:51.193
Since triangles A B C and F C E stand on equal bases B C and C E and lie in the same parallels B E and A F, by Proposition Thirty-Eight, triangle A B C equals triangle F C E.

9
00:00:51.193 --> 00:00:55.008
Yet by hypothesis, triangle A B C equals triangle D C E.

10
00:00:55.008 --> 00:01:06.657
By Common Notion One, things equal to the same thing are equal to one another, so triangle D C E equals triangle F C E, the greater equal to the less, which is impossible.

11
00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:14.151
Thus segment A F cannot be parallel to segment B E, nor can any other line through point A except segment A D.

12
00:01:14.151 --> 00:01:19.260
Therefore segment A D is parallel to segment B E, as was required to prove.
